feat(mcp): agents can finally see the user's servers in `cmdr://state` and `list_volumes`

`snapshot_volumes` built its own list from `crate::volumes::list_locations` plus a hand-appended MTP block, so no row
ever carried `fs_type: "sftp" | "webdav" | "adb"`. That made three arms of `kind_for_location` and every
`connectionState` past `disconnected` unreachable in practice, while `mcp/resources/DETAILS.md` advertised the widened
token sets as the agent-facing contract. The compiler couldn't see it: the arms are constructed inside a function that
IS called, just never with those inputs. `agent/tools/read/volumes.rs` maps the same snapshot, so `list_volumes` had the
same hole.

The macOS branch now runs `volume_listing::complete` through `list_with_timeout`, the same pipeline the `list_volumes`
IPC command and the `volumes-changed` push use, so a saved SFTP place reaches an agent as `kind: sftp` with
`connectionState: saved` and the agent can route a connect at it. `complete` also folds in every device provider's
storages, so the duplicated MTP block goes away on macOS; Linux keeps it, since its branch still lists only `root`.

Two details the shared pipeline needed:

- **`indexStatus` by path OR by id.** Path routing places a real mount point; a scheme root (`mtp://`, `sftp://`) is not
  a path any router can place, so those look up by volume id. That is what MTP's old hand-rolled block did, now stated
  as a rule rather than as a separate code path.
- **No `mountPath` on a scheme-rooted row.** A scheme root is not a path a `search` scope can take, and handing one over
  under that name would read as one. Preserves MTP's existing behavior and extends its reasoning to the servers.

Cell: a remembered SFTP server reaching the real snapshot with the right kind and state, driven through
`snapshot_volumes` rather than a fixture, because a fixture is exactly what stayed green through this.
